# CONNECT-NEXT-ADDRESS: a WebSocket to a name resolves it with the system resolver (uv_getaddrinfo, as fetch does) and
# tries every address it gives, in its order, while connect() fails, as native Bun's WebSocket and node's clients do.
#
# WHY. ws.c handed lws the name, and lws's own async DNS asked for A records only (connect2.c: LWS_ADNS_RECORD_A;
# "localhost" is synthesized as 127.0.0.1), so a WebSocket to localhost never reached a server on ::1, and reached a
# server on both families by its IPv4 address where native reaches ::1 first. fetch already resolved with uv_getaddrinfo
# (build-tjs.cjs fixupHttpclientAsyncDns), but tried the next address only when lws refused to START a connection, not
# when a connection was refused, so a fetch to localhost (::1 first) of a server on 127.0.0.1 only failed ECONNREFUSED
# where native connects (measured 2026-11-24 with the native Bun preload oracle: test/connect-family.test.cjs).
#
# WHAT. private.h/lws-utils.c: TJSLwsAddrs (the resolver's addresses, deduplicated, in order) and
# tjs__lws_connect_failed(reason): lws's CLIENT_CONNECTION_ERROR reason names a connect() that failed or timed out
# (connect3.c's "conn fail: ...", "Unable to connect", "incompatible v6 scopes"), so nothing was sent and the next
# address may be tried; a TLS or protocol failure is not retried. ws.c: the constructor keeps what an attempt needs
# (TJSWsDial), resolves the name connected to (the proxy's, through a caller's proxy), and dials address by address;
# CLIENT_CONNECTION_ERROR of a failed connect() goes on to the next address from a job; close() before the connection
# is made stops further attempts. An environment proxy's name is still resolved by lws, as before. fetch's half is in
# fixupHttpclientAsyncDns, which owns the resolve. vm.c: globalThis.__tjs_connect_next_address = true, floored by
# scripts/engine-api-floor.cjs. Last in TXIKI_PATCH_ORDER, diffed against the fully patched tree.
#
# NOT DONE. node's clients give each address but the last 250 ms (autoSelectFamilyAttemptTimeout) before the next
# begins; here an address that never answers holds the attempt for lws's connect timeout (the context's timeout_secs)
# before the next is tried. Native Bun's timing for that case was not measured (no name resolving to a blackhole and a
# live address could be made without editing the system resolver).
#
